<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donbas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Donbas is a region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and heavy industry in the basin of the Donets River, in present-day eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In the late nineteenth century it became one of the main centres of industrialisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, with rapid growth of coal mining and iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donbas.jpg" alt="Donbas" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shamil Khakirov from Ukraine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sviatohirsk_Cave_Monastery_in_Ukraine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Donbas lies in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> between the middle and lower Donets river in the north and the Azov Upland and Lowland in the south, across the border from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The core area, about 23,000 square kilometres, corresponds roughly to the Donets Ridge, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> seams reach the surface and which is known as the Old Donbas. Coal elsewhere lies at depths of 500 to 600 metres or more, in areas that became producing regions only after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nomadic peoples including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, Alans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> lived on this steppe for centuries. It was largely unpopulated until the second half of the 17th century, when Don Cossacks founded the first permanent settlements. The first town, Solanoye (now Soledar), was founded in 1676 for rock-salt working. The area, called the Wild Fields, lay under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> and the Crimean Khanate until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> conquered the one and annexed the other in the mid to late 18th century.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> was discovered in 1721 and exploited on a large scale from the mid to late 19th century, when the name Donbas came into use. Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> mills were set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luhansk">Luhansk</a> in 1795, near Yenakiieve in 1859 to 1864 and at Lysychansk in 1866 to 1870. The Welsh businessman John Hughes founded Yuzivka, later Donetsk, in 1869 and built a steel mill and collieries. Landless peasants came in large numbers to find work, and poor conditions led to strikes, including a wave in 1905.</p><p>The region was incorporated into the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic in 1919. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> output fell sharply between 1913 and 1920, and pre-war levels returned by 1928 to 1929. Under German occupation in 1941 to 1943, 279,000 civilians were killed in what is now Donetsk Oblast and 45,649 in Luhansk Oblast, and production of 1940 was restored only in 1949. After independence in 1991 industry declined sharply. In 2014 unrest in the region grew into a war that has continued within the wider Russo-Ukrainian war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Donbas is usually taken to mean Donetsk Oblast and Luhansk Oblast. Donetsk is considered its unofficial capital, and other large cities include Mariupol, Luhansk, Makiivka, Horlivka, Kramatorsk, Sloviansk, Alchevsk, Sievierodonetsk and Lysychansk. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2001 the population of the Ukrainian part was about 58 percent ethnic Ukrainian and 38 percent ethnic Russian. The region remains defined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donbas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This growth remained concentrated in Saint Petersburg, Moscow, the <b>Donbas</b> coalfields and the Baku oilfields, and left large agricultural regions largely unchanged.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk, German and Austro-Hungarian forces advanced into the <b>Donbas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/donetsk-krivoy-rog-soviet-republic">Donetsk-Krivoy Rog Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
